  "Question": "Let \\(F=0.4818181\\ldots\\) be an infinite repeating decimal with the digits \\(8\\) and \\(1\\) repeating. When \\(F\\) is written as a fraction in lowest terms, the denominator exceeds the numerator by:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(13\\)",
    "B": "\\(14\\)",
    "C": "\\(29\\)",
    "D": "\\(57\\)",
    "E": "\\(126\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The initial digit \\(4\\) does not repeat, so first move it to the integer part. Then shifting by one complete two-digit repeating block produces the same decimal tail:\n\\[\n10F=4.818181\\ldots,\n\\qquad\n1000F=481.818181\\ldots.\n\\]\nSubtracting cancels the entire repeating tail:\n\\[\n990F=477,\n\\qquad\nF=\\frac{477}{990}=\\frac{53}{110}.\n\\]\nThis fraction is in lowest terms: \\(110=2\\cdot5\\cdot11\\), and none of these prime factors divides \\(53\\). Therefore the denominator exceeds the numerator by\n\\[\n110-53=57,\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
